### Description

The Bedrock provider currently requires explicitly defining either the AWS credentials or the environment variables `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, and (optionally) `AWS_SESSION_TOKEN` when using `createAmazonBedrock()` instead of relying on Amazon's internal credential chains. This makes it nearly impossible to use with assumed roles through SSO profiles, instance roles, or container roles in ECS or EKS since those variables would have to be defined, and then updated, at runtime.

Instead of forcing a lookup from `process.env` in the options, and failing if undefined, pass those through to the `BedrockRuntimeClient` and let it handle credential resolution natively. It will use those values if you provide them, falling back to the `AWS_*` environment variables, then `AWS_PROFILE`, then container role lookup, then instance role lookup, then failure.

### Expected Behavior

The Bedrock provider uses AWS' native credential chain to resolve access from any supported credential provider, calls Bedrock, and returns the response from the model.

### Actual Behavior

`bedrock` and `createAmazonBedrock` functions throw an exception if credentials are not explicitly provided using environment variables or the options object.

### Code example

```
