## Context
I have multiple agents working together and want to minimize memory usage by taking advantage of Node/Bun's streaming capabilities rather than writing buffering code myself. I'm streaming outputs between agents over HTTP.
## The issue
When the AI SDK is misconfigured (e.g., missing API key) or encounters other errors:

1. In my case: HTTP headers have already been written (resulting in a 200 status code with no response body)
Since the stream has been passed to the underlying HTTP handler, I can't write the error message to the response
2. Since these errors typically happen on the first read to textStream, it would be nice if `streamText` could just call that first read for me and throw that error so i can deal with it and return a correct error code.

If thats too big a hurdle (or doesnt make sense to do) it would also work if i could ask the framework to write the error message directly to the readable stream. This sounds crazy but in an agentic world it actually works pretty well, the receiving agent will handle it ok, and the error will likely get surfaced one way or another.

Maybe theres some way around this I'm not thinking of, but AFAIK you gotta buffer some chunk of the response, while intermittently checking for errors in order to get a safeStream that you can pass on to some other low-level component.

