## Workarounds

We're experimenting with `useChat`'s `onToolCall` + `onFinish` callbacks for manually detecting + cleaning up some local state for "active tool calls". But the UX is janky at best and misleading at worst:

```typescript
const [activeToolCalls, setActiveToolCalls] = useState<ToolCall<string, any>[]>([]);

const chat = useChat({
    maxSteps: 25,
    api: '/api/chat',
    body: {model: selectedModel.value},
    initialMessages: [DEFAULT_ASSISTANT_MESSAGE],
    onFinish: (message) => {
      const completedToolCallIds = message.parts
        ?.filter((part) => part.type === 'tool-invocation')
        ?.filter((part) => part.toolInvocation.state === 'result')
        ?.map(({toolInvocation}) => toolInvocation.toolCallId);

      setActiveToolCalls((calls) => calls.filter(
        (call) => !completedToolCallIds?.includes(call.toolCallId))
      );
    },
    onToolCall: async ({toolCall}) => {
      setActiveToolCalls((calls) => [...calls, toolCall]);
    },
  });
```

