### Description

# Summary
There's an inconsistency in how the loading state is managed when processing start chunks in the UI message stream. The write callback defined in runUpdateMessageJob is only called in updateMessageMetadata for start chunks when the message doesn't have both id and metadata, leading to inconsistent status transitions.

**Affected Files**
[packages/ai/src/ui/chat.ts](https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/1c1f6caec0903f88a34de9c55530ee3ed680b86f/packages/ai/src/ui/chat.ts#L540C7-L540C12)
[packages/ai/src/ui/process-ui-message-stream.ts](https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/1c1f6caec0903f88a34de9c55530ee3ed680b86f/packages/ai/src/ui/process-ui-message-stream.ts#L602)

# Current Behavior
## Scenario 1: Start chunk WITH `messageId` OR `messageMetadata`

```typescript
case 'start': {
  if (chunk.messageId != null) {
    state.message.id = chunk.messageId;
  }

  await updateMessageMetadata(chunk.messageMetadata);

  if (chunk.messageId != null || chunk.messageMetadata != null) {
    write(); // ✅ write() is called
  }
  break;
}
```
**Status transitions:** `submitted` → `streaming` (immediately)
**Loading state:** Stops immediately when start chunk is received

## Scenario 2: Start chunk WITHOUT `messageId` AND without `messageMetadata`

```typescript
case 'start': {
  if (chunk.messageId != null) { // false
    state.message.id = chunk.messageId;
  }

  await updateMessageMetadata(chunk.messageMetadata); // chunk.messageMetadata is null/undefined

  if (chunk.messageId != null || chunk.messageMetadata != null) { // false
    write(); // ❌ write() is NOT called
  }
  break;
}
```
**Status transitions:** Remains `submitted` until next chunk type arrives
**Loading** state: Continues showing loading until `text-start` or other chunk arrives

## Impact
The status should only transition from submitted to streaming when there is actually streaming content arriving (text, reasoning, tool calls, etc.), not just when metadata or message IDs are present in the start chunk.
The current behavior in Scenario 2 (where loading continues until actual content arrives) appears to be the more logical and expected behavior.

This inconsistency affects the user experience by showing different loading behaviors depending on whether the AI response includes metadata or message IDs in the initial chunk. Users might see:
- *Inconsistent loading indicators:* Loading stops immediately but stills there is a await time until the LLM actually starts sending data.
- *Developer confusion:* When implementing custom metadata or evolving the sdk examples to a more complex use-case the loading behavior starts to become unpredictable.


