### Description

Using a simple example with a `never` input (i.e. a function which just returns some current state to the LLM):
```typescript
import { tool, ToolCallOptions, ToolSet } from "ai";
import z from "zod";

const toolkit = {
  weather: tool({
    description: "Get the weather in a location (fahrenheit)",
    inputSchema: z.never(),
    execute: async (_input: never, _options: ToolCallOptions) => {
      const temperature = Math.round(Math.random() * (90 - 32) + 32);
      return {
        location: "London",
        temperature
      };
    }
  })
} satisfies ToolSet;
```

This errors out during build ultimately because the `execute` function takes `never` as the input. (It errors out similarly if you leave the arguments omitted or set them to undefined.)

This is because of the definition of `ToolSet`:
```typescript
type ToolSet = Record<string, (Tool<never, never> | Tool<any, any> | Tool<any, never> | Tool<never, any>) & Pick<Tool<any, any>, 'execute' | 'onInputAvailable' | 'onInputStart' | 'onInputDelta'>>;
```

Even though this clearly allows `never` as an input or output in the initial definition, the additional requirement that the function include an `execute` implementation that is compatible with `any` as both input and output means the implementation is ruled invalid when you try to pass it to the calls like `streamText`.

This goes away if you define a non-never input type:
```ts
import { tool, ToolSet } from "ai";
import z from "zod";

const toolkit = {
  weather: tool({
    description: "Get the weather in a location (fahrenheit)",
    inputSchema: z.object({
      location: z.string().describe("The location to get the weather for")
    }),
    execute: async ({ location }) => {
      const temperature = Math.round(Math.random() * (90 - 32) + 32);
      return {
        location,
        temperature
      };
    }
  })
} satisfies ToolSet;
```

I don't know the original motivation for adding the `& Pick` constraint to `ToolSet` so I'm not comfortable YOLO opening a PR for this. Let me know your thoughts and what can be done to refine this.

