### Description

From the [docs](https://ai-sdk.dev/docs/ai-sdk-ui/streaming-data#transient-data-parts-ephemeral):
> Transient parts are sent to the client but not added to the message history. They are only accessible via the `onData` useChat handler:

Despite the fact that transient parts are not added to the message history, it's required to define our own custom `UIMessage` with that transient part's name in the `DATA_PARTS` type parameter to gain type safety for transient parts written in the UI message stream:
```ts
const myMessagePersistentDataSchema = z.object({
  weather: z.object({
    city: z.string(),
    status: z.enum([...]),
  }),
});
type MyMessagePersistentData = z.infer<typeof myMessagePersistentDataSchema>;

const myTransientDataSchema = z.object({
  notification: z.object({
    message: z.string(),
    level: z.enum([...]),
  }),
});
type MyTransientData = z.infer<typeof myTransientDataSchema>;

type MyMessage = UIMessage<
  MyMessageMetadata,
  MyMessagePersistentData & MyTransientData,
  MyUITools
>;

// server
writer.write({
  type: 'data-notification',
  data: { message: 'Processing...', level: 'info' },
  transient: true,
});

// client
const [notification, setNotification] = useState();

const { messages } = useChat({
  onData: ({ data, type }) => {
    if (type === 'data-notification') {
      setNotification({ message: data.message, level: data.level });
    }
  },
});
```

This causes bad DX where you have to either unnecessarily handle or disable lint rules elsewhere in the code where transient data parts are irrelevant.
Take for example the [message mapping util from the persistence example repo](https://github.com/vercel-labs/ai-sdk-persistence-db/blob/main/lib/utils/message-mapping.ts). In this example we have a `switch` with `case`s covering (almost) all part types. When using transient data parts, there's no need to add a `case` for this part in this `switch`. But when using [`@typescript-eslint/switch-exhaustiveness-check`](https://typescript-eslint.io/rules/switch-exhaustiveness-check/), you'd be required to add an unnecessary `case` for `"data-notification"` despite it not being meant to be persisted! Sure, a workaround could be to enable the rule's `considerDefaultExhaustiveForUnions` option, or to disable the rule for that `switch`, but that's counterproductive.

Since transient parts are not related to the message whatsoever, it doesn't make sense for them to have to be passed in the type parameters of `UIMessage` & `UIMessagePart` in order to add type safety.

My suggestion is for transient data parts to either be:
1. separated from `UIMessage`/`UIMessagePart` into `createUIMessageStream()`/`createUIMessageStreamResponse()`, `toUIMessageStream()` and obviously `useChat()`, or
2. passed in a separate type parameter in `UIMessage`/`UIMessagePart`

