### Description

When streaming text, I want to run some code whenever a tool throws an error. For example:

```ts
const result = streamText({
  model: 'openai/gpt-4.1',
  tools: {
    sayHello: {
      inputSchema: z.object({}),
      execute: () => {
        throw new Error('oops!');
        return 'Hello!';
      },
    },
  },
  prompt: 'Say hello',
  onChunk: ({ chunk }) => {
    if (chunk.type === 'tool-error') {
      console.error('Tool failed', chunk.error);
    }
  }}
});
```

This does not work because `onChunk` does not receive `tool-error` parts. Reference: https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/2e860820bb56fe696fbc8003dffbbfffe840f97c/packages/ai/src/generate-text/stream-text.ts#L686-L697

The `onError` callback also does not receive `tool-error` parts. It only receives `error` parts. Reference: https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/2e860820bb56fe696fbc8003dffbbfffe840f97c/packages/ai/src/generate-text/stream-text.ts#L699-L701

The best solution I could find is using the `onStepFinish` callback and scanning the `content` parts for tool errors.

```ts
const result = streamText({
  model: 'openai/gpt-4.1',
  tools: {
    sayHello: {
      inputSchema: z.object({}),
      execute: () => {
        throw new Error('oops!');
        return 'Hello!';
      },
    },
  },
  prompt: 'Say hello',
  onStepFinish: ({ content }) => {
    content
      .filter((part) => part.type === 'tool-error')
      .forEach((part) => console.error('Tool failed', part.error))
  }
});
```

Is this the best way to handle this use case? Is there a reason why `onChunk` does not receive `tool-error`? I would prefer to do my processing in `onChunk` immediately after the tool error part is generated, rather than waiting for the step to finish.

