### Description

In the current Svelte implementation, `SvelteChatState.replaceMessage` reassigns the entire message object at the target index.

https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/c370b49e6a0a3e0eaa07f8c4399175e9c9825253/packages/svelte/src/chat.svelte.ts#L46-L48

As a result, even parts of the message that have not actually changed receive new object references. Thanks to Svelte’s fine-grained reactivity, unchanged parts are not re-rendered in the DOM, but the underlying update checks and reactive evaluations (often referred to as dirty checking or diffing) are still performed.

This is not a problem in common simple cases, for example when a response contains only a single text part.

However, it can become a performance issue when a response includes multiple parallel tool calls and a large number of `tool-input-delta` updates are streamed back to the client. Each time a single `tool-input-delta` arrives, the entire message is replaced, causing all parts of the message to be involved in the update checks, even though only a small portion has changed.

If updates were applied only to the modified portion within the message, Svelte’s fine-grained reactivity would allow the update work to be limited strictly to the changed data, avoiding unnecessary processing.

At the same time, trying to cover all possible update patterns inside the SDK would be complex and could be considered a breaking change. Many users do not need this level of control.

Providing an option that allows developers who are familiar with both Svelte and the Vercel AI SDK to customize this behavior would make it possible to support advanced use cases without changing the default behavior for everyone.

For example, how about adding an optional argument such as `experimental_stateFactory`, as shown below?

```ts

export type SvelteChatStateFactory<UI_MESSAGE extends UIMessage> = (
  messages?: UI_MESSAGE[],
) => ChatState<UI_MESSAGE>;

export class Chat<
  UI_MESSAGE extends UIMessage = UIMessage,
> extends AbstractChat<UI_MESSAGE> {
  constructor(
    init: ChatInit<UI_MESSAGE>,
    experimental_stateFactory?: SvelteChatStateFactory<UI_MESSAGE>,
  ) {
    super({
      ...init,
      state: experimental_stateFactory
        ? experimental_stateFactory(init.messages)
        : new SvelteChatState(init.messages),
    });
  }
}
```

Users could then use it like this:

```ts
/// customSvelteChatState.svelte.ts

class CustomSvelteChatState<UI_MESSAGE extends UIMessage> extends SvelteChatState<UI_MESSAGE> {
  constructor(messages: UI_MESSAGE[] = []) {
    super(messages);

    this.replaceMessage = (index: number, message: UI_MESSAGE) => {
		// Custom logic for replacing message
    };
  }
}

export const chatStateFactory = <UI_MESSAGE extends UIMessage>(
    messages?: UI_MESSAGE[],
) => {
    return new CustomSvelteChatState<UI_MESSAGE>(messages);
};
```

```ts
/// +page.svelte

import { chatStateFactory } from './customSvelteChatState.svelte'; 

let chat = new Chat(
	{  ... },
	chatStateFactory
)
```

