### Description

The `timestamp_granularities` property in transcription APIs means that there will be an iterable returned for each element in the array (assuming things are passed properly). 

While users can easily access both iterables (words and segments) through the full responses body, the groq implementation lacks parity with openai.

````
const result = await transcribe({
  model: groq.transcription('whisper-large-v3'),
  audio: await readFile(path.join('./sample_long.mp3')),
  providerOptions: {
    groq: {
      language: 'en',
      responseFormat: 'verbose_json',
      timestampGranularities: ['word'], // this is required for timestamp granularities
    },
  },
});

console.log('Segments:', result.segments);
````

The above code results in an empty result.segments, but for now the best solution may be to follow the openAI pattern and map words into segments for cases like this. That way users who never access the full response can still get a somewhat predictable result.segments that has parity with other providers.


Also, perhaps the DX should be more clearly suggesting users access the full response for both words and segments. It may require exposing the body property in the `transcription-model-response-metadata.ts` but I did not fully verify those typescript interactions. This could eliminate confusion until the team lands on a better solution. 

