### Description

When a tool's `execute()` throws, the SDK extracts the error via `getErrorMessage()` which uses `error.message`. This strips the "Error:" prefix,  making the tool result indistinguishable from a successful string return.    

[get-error-message.ts](https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/07d4ce6b134f50367dbaa4477eafc361dc1ceb64/packages/provider/src/errors/get-error-message.ts)
```ts
export function getErrorMessage(error: unknown | undefined) {
  if (error == null) {
    return 'unknown error';
  }

  if (typeof error === 'string') {
    return error;
  }

  if (error instanceof Error) {
    return error.message;
  }

  return JSON.stringify(error);
}

const error = new Error("API crashed");
// Current: "API crashed"
error.message    
// Proposed: "Error: API crashed"
error.toString()
```

The model receives "API crashed" as a plain `function_call_output` — there is  no signal that this was an error. 
Switching to `error.toString()` would at least preserve the error type prefix (Error, TypeError, RangeError, etc.), giving the model a hint that something went wrong.

