### Description

The Node-side auth helper used by `@ai-sdk/google-vertex` caches a module-level `GoogleAuth` instance, but invalidates that cache using object reference equality.

Current source:

```ts
// packages/google-vertex/src/google-vertex-auth-google-auth-library.ts
import { GoogleAuth, GoogleAuthOptions } from 'google-auth-library';

let authInstance: GoogleAuth | null = null;
let authOptions: GoogleAuthOptions | null = null;

function getAuth(options: GoogleAuthOptions) {
  if (!authInstance || options !== authOptions) {
    authInstance = new GoogleAuth({
      scopes: ['https://www.googleapis.com/auth/cloud-platform'],
      ...options,
    });
    authOptions = options;
  }
  return authInstance;
}

export async function generateAuthToken(options?: GoogleAuthOptions) {
  const auth = getAuth(options || {});
  const client = await auth.getClient();
  const token = await client.getAccessToken();
  return token?.token || null;
}
```

This creates two separate problems.

#### 1. Multiple provider instances with equivalent `googleAuthOptions` thrash the singleton

If an application creates multiple `createVertex()` providers with the same credentials but different `googleAuthOptions` object references, alternating between those providers causes `GoogleAuth` to be recreated.

This is because the Node provider captures `options.googleAuthOptions` and passes it directly to `generateAuthToken()`:

```ts
// packages/google-vertex/src/google-vertex-provider-node.ts
return createVertexOriginal({
  ...options,
  headers: async () => ({
    Authorization: `Bearer ${await generateAuthToken(
      options.googleAuthOptions,
    )}`,
    ...(await resolve(options.headers)),
  }),
});
```

A single `createVertex()` call reused across multiple models does **not** reproduce this specific case, because it reuses the same captured `googleAuthOptions` reference. The problem appears when applications create multiple provider instances, which is a common pattern in provider/model caches.

#### 2. `undefined` options recreate `GoogleAuth` on every call

When `googleAuthOptions` is omitted, `generateAuthToken()` calls:

```ts
getAuth(options || {})
```

That creates a new empty object every time `options` is `undefined`, so:

```ts
options !== authOptions
```

is always true after the first call.

As a result, even a single provider instance with no explicit `googleAuthOptions` can recreate `GoogleAuth` on every request.

### Scope

This appears to affect the shared Node auth helper used by:

- `@ai-sdk/google-vertex`
- `@ai-sdk/google-vertex/anthropic`
- `@ai-sdk/google-vertex/maas`

It does **not** appear to be the same issue in the Edge auth path, which uses a different implementation.

### Reproduction

#### Reproduction A: multiple provider instances

```ts
import { createVertex } from '@ai-sdk/google-vertex';
import { streamText } from 'ai';

const flash = createVertex({
  project: 'my-project',
  location: 'us-central1',
  googleAuthOptions: {
    credentials: {
      client_email: '...',
      private_key: '...',
    },
    scopes: ['https://www.googleapis.com/auth/cloud-platform'],
  },
});

const pro = createVertex({
  project: 'my-project',
  location: 'us-central1',
  googleAuthOptions: {
    credentials: {
      client_email: '...',
      private_key: '...',
    },
    scopes: ['https://www.googleapis.com/auth/cloud-platform'],
  },
});

await streamText({ model: flash('gemini-2.5-flash'), prompt: 'hi' });
await streamText({ model: pro('gemini-2.5-pro'), prompt: 'hi' });
// switching providers passes a different googleAuthOptions object reference
```

#### Reproduction B: omitted `googleAuthOptions`

```ts
import { createVertex } from '@ai-sdk/google-vertex';
import { streamText } from 'ai';

const vertex = createVertex({
  project: 'my-project',
  location: 'us-central1',
});

await streamText({ model: vertex('gemini-2.5-flash'), prompt: '1' });
await streamText({ model: vertex('gemini-2.5-flash'), prompt: '2' });
// generateAuthToken(undefined) -> getAuth({}) -> new {} each call
```

### Why this matters

`GoogleAuth` caches the resolved auth client on the instance, so recreating the `GoogleAuth` object also drops that cached client state. In environments using workload identity federation or other nontrivial auth flows, this can add measurable overhead.

In our production usage, we observed a large latency difference when requests alternated between separately-created provider instances with equivalent credentials. I’m not claiming the SDK source alone proves a specific latency number, but it does appear to explain the mechanism.

### Suggested fix

The simplest fix seems to be removing the module-level singleton and instead creating one `GoogleAuth` per provider instance.

For example, replace the shared helper shape with a factory:

```ts
import { GoogleAuth, GoogleAuthOptions } from 'google-auth-library';

export function createAuthTokenGenerator(options?: GoogleAuthOptions) {
  const auth = new GoogleAuth({
    scopes: ['https://www.googleapis.com/auth/cloud-platform'],
    ...options,
  });

  return async function generateAuthToken() {
    const client = await auth.getClient();
    const token = await client.getAccessToken();
    return token?.token ?? null;
  };
}
```

Then in the Node provider:

```ts
const generateAuthToken = createAuthTokenGenerator(options.googleAuthOptions);

return createVertexOriginal({
  ...options,
  headers: async () => ({
    Authorization: `Bearer ${await generateAuthToken()}`,
    ...(await resolve(options.headers)),
  }),
});
```

This would:

- avoid reference-equality invalidation entirely
- fix the `undefined` / `options || {}` problem
- give each provider instance stable auth/client caching
- avoid needing structural serialization of `GoogleAuthOptions` values such as `authClient`

### Suggested tests

- repeated calls with omitted `googleAuthOptions` should not recreate `GoogleAuth`
- repeated calls on one provider with explicit `googleAuthOptions` should not recreate `GoogleAuth`
- alternating between two separately-created providers should not cause unnecessary recreation within a provider
- same fix should cover the shared helper consumers in `anthropic` and `maas`

### Environment

We originally noticed the behavior while using Node.js with workload identity federation, but the cache invalidation logic appears auth-method-agnostic.

