### Description

## Problem

`createSigV4FetchFunction` and `createApiKeyFetchFunction` both use a JS default parameter:

```ts
fetch: FetchFunction = globalThis.fetch
```

This evaluates `globalThis.fetch` the moment `createAmazonBedrock()` is called. Any patch to `globalThis.fetch` applied after provider initialization is silently ignored.

## Expected Behavior

Match the pattern used by every other provider in the SDK — read `globalThis.fetch` lazily at request time via `getOriginalFetch()` in `post-to-api.ts`, so patches (e.g. from test frameworks, polyfills, or middleware) are always respected.

## Impact

The most common real-world case is **telemetry instrumentation** (e.g. OpenTelemetry, Datadog, custom tracing) which patches `globalThis.fetch` at startup to intercept outgoing HTTP requests. Because Bedrock snapshots fetch before those patches are applied, all Bedrock requests silently bypass instrumentation. Test setups that patch `globalThis.fetch` after imports and runtime environments that install a fetch polyfill after module load are equally affected.

## Affected Files

- `packages/amazon-bedrock/src/bedrock-sigv4-fetch.ts` — `createSigV4FetchFunction` (line 27) and `createApiKeyFetchFunction` (line 116)

## Proposed Fix

Remove the default parameter and resolve `globalThis.fetch` lazily inside the returned closure, matching the pattern used in `http-chat-transport.ts` (which carries the comment *"avoid caching globalThis.fetch in case it is patched by other libraries"*):

```ts
// before
export function createSigV4FetchFunction(
  getCredentials: ...,
  fetch: FetchFunction = globalThis.fetch,
): FetchFunction {
  return async (input, init) => {
    // fetch is already bound to the captured value
    return fetch(input, { ... });
  };
}

// after
export function createSigV4FetchFunction(
  getCredentials: ...,
  fetch?: FetchFunction,
): FetchFunction {
  return async (input, init) => {
    // avoid caching globalThis.fetch in case it is patched by other libraries
    const effectiveFetch = fetch ?? globalThis.fetch;
    return effectiveFetch(input, { ... });
  };
}
```

Apply the same change to `createApiKeyFetchFunction`.


