## Bug Report

### Description

In `packages/ai/src/generate-text/smooth-stream.ts`, the `smoothStream` transform silently drops `providerMetadata` when it detects word/line boundaries and emits buffered chunks via the chunking while loop.

### Root Cause

The `while` loop that emits chunked content does **not** include `providerMetadata` in the enqueued stream parts:

```typescript
// Lines 153-158 in smooth-stream.ts
while ((match = detectChunk(buffer)) != null) {
  controller.enqueue({ type, text: match, id }); // providerMetadata is dropped!
  buffer = buffer.slice(match.length);
  await delay(delayInMs);
}
```

However, the `flushBuffer` function **does** correctly include `providerMetadata`:

```typescript
// Lines 117-122 in smooth-stream.ts
controller.enqueue({
  type,
  text: buffer,
  id,
  ...(providerMetadata != null ? { providerMetadata } : {}), // included here
});
```

### Impact

When `smoothStream` is used with providers that embed metadata in streaming deltas (e.g., Anthropic extended thinking / reasoning blocks which carry `providerMetadata` with thinking signatures), that metadata is silently dropped for every chunk emitted from the chunking `while` loop. Only the very last remaining content (emitted via `flushBuffer`) retains the metadata.

This means:
- Anthropic extended thinking signatures are lost from intermediate stream parts
- Any provider-specific metadata attached to `text-delta` or `reasoning-delta` chunks is silently dropped when `smoothStream` is active

### Steps to Reproduce

```typescript
import { streamText } from "ai";
import { anthropic } from "@ai-sdk/anthropic";
import { smoothStream } from "ai";

const result = streamText({
  model: anthropic("claude-3-7-sonnet-20250219", {
    thinking: { type: "enabled", budgetTokens: 5000 },
  }),
  prompt: "Explain step by step how addition works.",
  experimental_transform: smoothStream({ chunking: "word" }),
});

for await (const part of result.fullStream) {
  if (part.type === "reasoning-delta") {
    // providerMetadata (thinking signatures) will be undefined for most chunks
    console.log(part.providerMetadata); // undefined for word-boundary chunks
  }
}
```

### Expected Behavior

All emitted stream parts from `smoothStream` should preserve `providerMetadata` from the original chunk that contained it.

### Actual Behavior

`providerMetadata` is `undefined` for all chunks emitted from the chunking `while` loop. Only the final flushed remainder chunk (via `flushBuffer`) may carry `providerMetadata`.

### Fix

Include `providerMetadata` in the `controller.enqueue` call within the `while` loop:

```typescript
while ((match = detectChunk(buffer)) != null) {
  controller.enqueue({
    type,
    text: match,
    id,
    ...(providerMetadata != null ? { providerMetadata } : {}),
  });
  buffer = buffer.slice(match.length);
  await delay(delayInMs);
}
```

### Environment

- Package: `ai` (latest)
- File: `packages/ai/src/generate-text/smooth-stream.ts`
- Affected providers: Any provider that attaches `providerMetadata` to `text-delta` or `reasoning-delta` stream parts (e.g., Anthropic extended thinking)

### Description

I'd like to propose adding [`TracingChannel`](https://nodejs.org/api/diagnostics_channel.html#class-tracingchannel) support to the Vercel AI SDK as the native observability primitive, following the pattern established by [`undici`](https://github.com/nodejs/undici) in Node.js core and adopted across the npm ecosystem.

The AI SDK already has excellent built-in telemetry via `@opentelemetry/api`. This proposal isn't about adding observability from scratch. It's about fixing how that observability gets activated and making it work reliably across all runtimes.

### The problem today

The SDK's telemetry requires per-call opt-in:

```ts
const result = await generateText({
  model: openai('gpt-4o'),
  prompt: 'Hello',
  experimental_telemetry: { isEnabled: true }, // required on every call
});
```

APM tools like Sentry work around this by using IITM (import-in-the-middle) to intercept `require('ai')` and auto-enable telemetry on every call. This has several problems:

- **Runtime lock-in:** IITM relies on Node.js-specific module loader internals. It doesn't work on Cloudflare Workers, Deno, or Bun. The AI SDK is designed to run on **all of these runtimes**, making IITM especially inadequate.
- **ESM fragility:** IITM is built on Node.js's module customization hooks, which are still evolving and have been a persistent source of breakage in the OTel JS ecosystem.
- **Initialization ordering:** IITM requires instrumentation to be set up before the SDK is first imported. Get the order wrong and instrumentation silently does nothing.
- **Bundling and Externalization:** Users have to ensure `ai` is externalized from their bundle, which is increasingly difficult with frameworks that bundle server-side code.

The result: on Cloudflare Workers, users must manually add `experimental_telemetry: { isEnabled: true }` to every AI call. Forget one and that call is invisible to your APM. Sentry maintains [two completely separate integration paths](https://github.com/getsentry/sentry-javascript/blob/7c193254674cebba2b6241cc0abd94b60c4a0235/packages/cloudflare/src/integrations/tracing/vercelai.ts) for Node vs Cloudflare because of this.

### Adopting Diagnostic/Tracing Channels

With `diagnostics_channel`, the SDK emits structured lifecycle events automatically, gated by `hasSubscribers` (zero cost when nobody is listening). APM tools become subscribers, not patches. No IITM, no per-call opt-in, no per-runtime code paths.

I'm thinking we can start with `vercel:ai:generate` and `vercel:ai:embed` channels that emit structured data that is typically used in instrumentations (mostly sourced from args) like : `model` and `params` from which APMs can extract messages, temperature, and other tuning or configuration values per call.

I'm aware that the SDK currently ships its own telemetry via direct `@opentelemetry/api` usage. TracingChannel complements this by providing a lower-level primitive which then can be used to power the OTEL behavior:

```ts
import dc from 'node:diagnostics_channel';

dc.tracingChannel('vercel:ai:generate').subscribe({
  start(ctx) {
    // ctx.method, ctx.model, ctx.stream, ctx.params available
    ctx.span = tracer.startSpan(`${ctx.method} ${ctx.model.modelId}`);
  },
  asyncEnd(ctx) {
    // ctx.result is auto-set by TracingChannel with the function result
    ctx.span?.end();
  },
  error(ctx) {
    ctx.span?.recordException(ctx.error);
  },
});
```

Adopting tracing channels doesn't just offer an OTEL-less way of instrumentation it also enables a cross-runtime interoperability for all the popular runtimes today including bun, deno and cloudflare workers as tracing channels is supported across all of these runtimes.

We can spec this further in a PR, I will be happy to submit one and work with your team on getting it to the finish line. I work for Sentry and we are driving an initiative across the ecosystem to get as many popular libraries in the ecosystem to adopt the tracing channel API.

## Backward Compatability

This can be added in a minor release since it wouldn't break anything for anyone, the main idea is to add tracing channels which is then used by the opentelemetry integration you folks have today to emit the OTEL telemetry.

Otherwise those channels remain in the background, still available for subscribers which then would have zero cost until subscribed to by an APM or by the user.

## Prior Art

This approach follows the same pattern already adopted or in progress by other libraries:

**AI / ML:**
- **`openai`**: [openai/openai-node#1819](https://github.com/openai/openai-node/issues/1819), issue opened

**Others:**
- **`undici`** (Node.js core): ships `TracingChannel` support since Node 20.12 ([`undici:request`](https://nodejs.org/api/diagnostics_channel.html#undici-channels))
- **`fastify`**: ships `TracingChannel` support natively (`tracing:fastify.request.handler`)
- **`h3`**: [h3js/h3#1251](https://github.com/h3js/h3/pull/1251) 
- **`srvx`**: [h3js/srvx#141](https://github.com/h3js/srvx/pull/141) 
- **`mysql2`**: [sidorares/node-mysql2#4178](https://github.com/sidorares/node-mysql2/pull/4178) 
- **`node-redis`**: [redis/node-redis#3195](https://github.com/redis/node-redis/pull/3195) 
- **`ioredis`**: [redis/ioredis#2089](https://github.com/redis/ioredis/pull/2089)
- **`unstorage`**: [unjs/unstorage#707](https://github.com/unjs/unstorage/pull/707) 



