### Description

## Summary

When using `@ai-sdk/amazon-bedrock`, cache points attached to individual message content parts do not appear to be included in the generated Bedrock Converse request.

This affects clients that attach prompt caching metadata to content blocks rather than to the whole message. The request succeeds, but prompt caching does not activate: usage reports `cache_write=0` and `cache_read=0`.

## Scenario

A client sends a Claude model request through Bedrock with a long reusable prompt prefix and a message whose text block has a cache point.

Minimal setup used by the examples:

```ts
import { createAmazonBedrock } from '@ai-sdk/amazon-bedrock';
import { generateText } from 'ai';

// Assumes AWS credentials are available through the normal environment/default
// credential chain.
const bedrock = createAmazonBedrock({
  region: 'us-east-1',
});

const model = bedrock('us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-7');
const longReusableContext = 'This is reusable context. '.repeat(2000);
```

## What Works

Prompt caching works when the cache point is attached at the message level:

```ts
async function runMessageLevelCachePoint() {
  const result = await generateText({
    model,
    maxOutputTokens: 64,
    messages: [
      {
        role: 'user',
        content: [
          {
            type: 'text',
            text: `${longReusableContext}\n\nQuestion: What is 2 + 2?`,
          },
        ],
        providerOptions: {
          bedrock: {
            cachePoint: { type: 'default' },
          },
        },
      },
    ],
  });

  console.log(result.totalUsage.inputTokenDetails);
  return result.text;
}

await runMessageLevelCachePoint();
await runMessageLevelCachePoint();
```

In this case, Bedrock receives a `cachePoint` in the message content and usage reports cache writes/reads as expected.

Prompt caching also works for system prompt cache points:

```ts
async function runSystemCachePoint() {
  const result = await generateText({
    model,
    maxOutputTokens: 64,
    messages: [
      {
        role: 'system',
        content: longReusableContext,
        providerOptions: {
          bedrock: {
            cachePoint: { type: 'default' },
          },
        },
      },
      {
        role: 'user',
        content: 'What is 2 + 2?',
      },
    ],
  });

  console.log(result.totalUsage.inputTokenDetails);
  return result.text;
}

await runSystemCachePoint();
await runSystemCachePoint();
```

## What Does Not Work

Prompt caching does not work when the cache point is attached to an individual message content part:

```ts
async function runPartLevelCachePoint() {
  const result = await generateText({
    model,
    maxOutputTokens: 64,
    messages: [
      {
        role: 'user',
        content: [
          {
            type: 'text',
            text: `${longReusableContext}\n\nQuestion: What is 2 + 2?`,
            providerOptions: {
              bedrock: {
                cachePoint: { type: 'default' },
              },
            },
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
  });

  console.log(result.totalUsage.inputTokenDetails);
  return result.text;
}

await runPartLevelCachePoint();
await runPartLevelCachePoint();
```

The same issue appears to apply to assistant message content parts:

```ts
async function runAssistantPartLevelCachePoint() {
  const result = await generateText({
    model,
    maxOutputTokens: 64,
    messages: [
      {
        role: 'user',
        content: 'Remember the following reusable context.',
      },
      {
        role: 'assistant',
        content: [
          {
            type: 'text',
            text: longReusableContext,
            providerOptions: {
              bedrock: {
                cachePoint: { type: 'default' },
              },
            },
          },
        ],
      },
      {
        role: 'user',
        content: 'What is 2 + 2?',
      },
    ],
  });

  console.log(result.totalUsage.inputTokenDetails);
  return result.text;
}

await runAssistantPartLevelCachePoint();
await runAssistantPartLevelCachePoint();
```

The request completes, but Bedrock usage reports no cache activity:

```ts
{
  cacheWriteTokens: 0,
  cacheReadTokens: 0,
  noCacheTokens: /* all input tokens */
}
```

The expected shape would be:

```ts
{
  cacheWriteTokens: /* > 0 on first request */,
  cacheReadTokens: /* > 0 on repeated request */
}
```

## Expected Behavior

A cache point attached to a message content part should be sent to Bedrock as a `cachePoint` content block at that location.

This is supported by the [Bedrock Converse prompt caching docs](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/prompt-caching.html), which show `cachePoint` as an item inside `messages[].content[]`.



