### Description

When routing `streamText` through `@ai-sdk/gateway` to Vertex Gemini 3 with thinking enabled and tools, **the `providerMetadata.vertex.thoughtSignature` field is missing from `tool-call` events on the response stream**. `callProviderMetadata` is never set on saved tool parts, and the next agent step fails with:

> Function call is missing a thought_signature in functionCall parts. This is required for tools to work correctly...

Going direct via `@ai-sdk/google-vertex` (`createVertex(...)('gemini-3-flash-preview')`) on the same conversation does include `vertex.thoughtSignature` on tool calls and works.

The gateway *does* attach its own provider metadata to other event types: `reasoning-start` / `reasoning-delta` parts come back with `providerMetadata.gateway.generationId`. So this looks like a tool-call-specific drop on the gateway server side, not a generic passthrough issue. The client-side `gateway-language-model.ts` `transform` is a verbatim `controller.enqueue(streamPart)` with no field stripping.

### Steps to reproduce

```ts
import { gateway } from '@ai-sdk/gateway';
import { streamText, stepCountIs, tool } from 'ai';
import { z } from 'zod';

const result = streamText({
  model: gateway('google/gemini-3-flash-preview'),
  providerOptions: {
    gateway: { only: ['vertex'] },
    vertex: {
      streamFunctionCallArguments: true,
      thinkingConfig: { includeThoughts: true, thinkingLevel: 'medium' },
    },
  },
  tools: {
    read_screen: tool({
      description: 'Read a screen by id',
      inputSchema: z.object({ id: z.string() }),
      execute: async ({ id }) => ({ id, html: '<div/>' }),
    }),
  },
  stopWhen: stepCountIs(5),
  prompt: 'Read screens A, B, C, then summarize.',
});

for await (const part of result.fullStream) {
  if (part.type === 'tool-call') {
    console.log('tool-call providerMetadata:', part.providerMetadata);
    // -> undefined when routed via gateway
    // -> { vertex: { thoughtSignature: '...' } } when via createVertex direct
  }
}
```

The next step (after tool execution) POSTs to Vertex with `functionCall` parts that have no `thoughtSignature`, and Vertex returns 400.

### Expected

`tool-call` (and `tool-input-start` / `tool-input-end`) events emitted by `gateway('google/...')` with `gateway.only: ['vertex']` should include `providerMetadata.vertex.thoughtSignature` whenever the underlying Vertex response carries one, matching the behavior of `@ai-sdk/google-vertex`.

### Actual

`providerMetadata` on `tool-call` events is `undefined`. Saved UI message parts have `callProviderMetadata: undefined`. The next agent step has no signature to attach and Vertex 400s.

### Reproduced against real chats

Comparison from the same project DB, same conversation, same `gemini-3-flash-preview`:

| Path | Tool-call parts | `callProviderMetadata.vertex.thoughtSignature` |
|---|---|---|
| `createVertex(...)` direct | 6/6 (theme + 4 screens + image) | present on all |
| `gateway('google/gemini-3-flash-preview')` + `gateway.only: ['vertex']` | 0/4 (parallel `read_screen`) | absent on all |

### Environment

- `ai`: `6.0.168`
- `@ai-sdk/gateway`: `3.0.104`
- `@ai-sdk/google`: `3.0.64` (transitive type only)
- `@ai-sdk/provider`: `3.0.8`
- Auth: Vercel AI Gateway, `AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY` (no BYOK)
- Model: `google/gemini-3-flash-preview`

### Related

- #13908 propagates the *first* `thoughtSignature` to parallel `functionCall` parts. Necessary, but not sufficient for the gateway path: in our gateway repro, *zero* of the parallel calls have a signature, including the first one, so #13908's fallback has nothing to copy from.
- #14196 is a different gateway+Vertex failure mode (cross-provider failover with incompatible signatures), but suggests gateway↔Vertex signature handling has multiple gaps.
- #13429 is gateway-vs-vertex divergence on tool-input-delta streaming. Same general theme: gateway path drops or reshapes data that direct Vertex preserves.

When `streamFunctionCallArguments: true` is set and the model invokes a tool whose `inputSchema` has no required fields, the streaming parser drops the whole function-call chunk. Its `thoughtSignature` goes with it. For Gemini 3 thinking models this often breaks multi-turn conversations: Vertex emits one signature per response, on the first function call, and if that first call is a no-args call, the response message ends up with zero signatures. The next agent step then 400s.

This bug reproduces on **both** the `@ai-sdk/google` parser (direct Vertex path) and the AI Gateway server (`gateway('google/...')` path). They appear to be two copies of the same bug. Fixing only `@ai-sdk/google` will not fix the gateway path; the gateway server needs the same fix.

### Repro

```ts
import { createVertex } from '@ai-sdk/google-vertex';
import { streamText, tool } from 'ai';
import { z } from 'zod';

const vertex = createVertex({
  apiKey: process.env.GOOGLE_VERTEX_API_KEY!,
  project: '_',
  location: 'global',
});

const result = streamText({
  model: vertex('gemini-3-flash-preview'),
  tools: {
    read_theme: tool({
      description: 'Read the current theme.',
      inputSchema: z.object({}),
      execute: async () => ({ '--background': '#fff' }),
    }),
    read_screen: tool({
      description: 'Read a screen.',
      inputSchema: z.object({ id: z.string() }),
      execute: async ({ id }) => ({ id, html: '<div/>' }),
    }),
  },
  providerOptions: {
    vertex: {
      streamFunctionCallArguments: true,
      thinkingConfig: { includeThoughts: true, thinkingLevel: 'medium' },
    },
  },
  prompt: 'Read the theme and screens A, B, C in parallel, then summarize.',
});

for await (const part of result.fullStream) {
  if (part.type === 'tool-call') {
    console.log(part.toolName, part.providerMetadata);
  }
}
```

Expected: four `tool-call` events; the first one (`read_theme`) carries `providerMetadata.vertex.thoughtSignature`.

Actual: three `tool-call` events (`read_screen` only). No `read_theme`. No signature anywhere.

### Wire-level evidence

The Vertex SSE response for this scenario contains:

```
chunk 1: { functionCall: { name: 'read_theme' }, thoughtSignature: '<2KB>' }
chunk 2: { functionCall: { name: 'read_screen', willContinue: true } }
chunk 3..5: { functionCall: { partialArgs: [...], willContinue: true } }
chunk 6: { functionCall: {} }
... repeats for screens B, C ...
```

Vertex does send the signature, on the first call.

### Where it gets dropped (direct Vertex)

In [`packages/google/src/google-language-model.ts`](https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/main/packages/google/src/google-language-model.ts), the streaming branch around [L823-L835](https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/main/packages/google/src/google-language-model.ts#L823-L835) classifies each function-call chunk into one of three buckets:

```ts
const isStreamingChunk =
  part.functionCall.partialArgs != null ||
  (part.functionCall.name != null &&
    part.functionCall.willContinue === true);
const isTerminalChunk =
  part.functionCall.name == null &&
  part.functionCall.args == null &&
  part.functionCall.partialArgs == null &&
  part.functionCall.willContinue == null;
const isCompleteCall =
  part.functionCall.name != null &&
  part.functionCall.args != null &&
  part.functionCall.partialArgs == null;
```

A no-args complete call arrives as `{ name: 'X' }`, with no `args`, no `willContinue`, no `partialArgs`. None of the three classifiers match. The chunk falls through and is never emitted.

The non-streaming path at [L371-L374](https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/main/packages/google/src/google-language-model.ts#L371-L374) already filters with `part.functionCall.name != null && part.functionCall.args != null`, which also excludes no-args calls, but the streaming path is the one that matters when `streamFunctionCallArguments: true`.

### AI Gateway has the same bug

Running the same scenario through `gateway('google/gemini-3-flash-preview')` with `providerOptions.gateway: { only: ['vertex'] }` reproduces the same failure. We captured the raw v3 SSE stream the gateway returns to the client, and in failure runs there is no `tool-input-start`, `tool-input-end`, or `tool-call` event for `read_theme` anywhere. The whole call is missing from the v3 stream, along with any `vertex.thoughtSignature` it would have carried.

Direct Vertex always emits exactly one signature on the wire (on `read_theme` for that scenario). The gateway sometimes returns a v3 stream with that signature visible (when the model's first call has args) and sometimes returns a v3 stream with zero signatures (when the first call is the no-args `read_theme`). The split correlates exactly with the model's tool choice, which matches the parser-side bug pattern: when Vertex sends `{ name: 'X' }` with no `args`, both parsers drop it.

So this is two copies of the same bug, one in `@ai-sdk/google` (client-side) and one in the gateway server, and they need to be fixed together. A client-side `@ai-sdk/google` fix alone leaves the gateway path broken because the v3 stream is already missing the call by the time the client sees it.

### Additional gateway sig-dropping behavior (separate from the no-args bug)

The no-args bug above is the primary, sharply-isolated problem. The gateway has at least one additional sig-dropping behavior we cannot isolate as cleanly, but observed both in our test captures and in production traffic.

**Production observation**: an assistant turn produced through `gateway('google/gemini-3-flash-preview')` containing a single `read_screen` call (a streamed-args tool, not no-args) was saved with `callProviderMetadata: { gateway: { generationId: '...' } }` and no `vertex` namespace at all. The next agent step 400'd with the same `missing thought_signature` error. The model never picked the no-args tool, so the workaround above did not apply.

**Test capture**: in one of our gateway captures (15 attempts), one failure had a v3 SSE stream with zero `vertex.thoughtSignature` across all four streamed parallel `read_screen` calls, none were no-args.

We don't have the underlying Vertex wire for those requests, so we can't say definitively whether Vertex didn't emit a sig that round or whether the gateway dropped it on the way out. Direct Vertex's behavior across our captures has been to emit exactly one sig per response (on the first function call, always). For the gateway we sometimes see zero sigs in the v3 stream, which is consistent with "gateway dropped them" rather than "Vertex didn't emit them".

Either way, fixing the no-args bug on `@ai-sdk/google` and the gateway server side is necessary but not sufficient for the gateway path. There appears to be at least one more source of sig drops in the gateway → v3 → client pipeline that we can't see from outside. A gateway-team look at sig propagation through that pipeline would be useful.

### Why this matters

For Gemini 3 with thinking enabled, Vertex enforces a rule on multi-turn conversations: an assistant turn that contains any function calls must contain at least one with a `thoughtSignature`, or it returns a 400 with `Unable to submit request because function call X in the N. content block is missing a 'thought_signature'`. Vertex emits one signature per response, on the first function call. If the parser drops that first call, the response message has zero signatures and the agent's next step trips the rule.

We see this on roughly half of all multi-turn chat continuations against an existing Gemini 3 project, depending on whether the model picks the no-args tool first.

### Workaround

Give every tool at least one **required** argument:

```ts
read_theme: tool({
  inputSchema: z.object({
    reason: z.string().describe('Why the theme is being read'),
  }),
  execute: async () => ({ ... }),
}),
```

`.optional()` is not enough. We initially shipped an optional variant and hit the same 400 in production: when the model decides to omit the optional arg (model choice, sensitive to the system prompt and history), Vertex emits the call as `{ "name": "X" }` with no args, no willContinue, no partialArgs. That hits the same drop path documented above.

A required arg forces the model to populate args, which puts Vertex on the streaming path (`{ "name": "X", "willContinue": true }` followed by `partialArgs` chunks), which the SDK handles. Confirmed with a wire-level test and 10/10 success on the previously-failing scenario, both on `createVertex` direct and `gateway('google/...')` paths.

### Proposed fix

After the existing `isCompleteCall` branch in [google-language-model.ts](https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/main/packages/google/src/google-language-model.ts#L923), add a fourth branch for the no-args case:

```ts
} else if (
  part.functionCall.name != null &&
  part.functionCall.args == null &&
  part.functionCall.willContinue !== true &&
  part.functionCall.partialArgs == null
) {
  // Single-chunk no-args call: emit as a complete call with empty args.
  const toolCallId = generateId();
  controller.enqueue({
    type: 'tool-input-start',
    id: toolCallId,
    toolName: part.functionCall.name,
    providerMetadata: providerMeta,
  });
  controller.enqueue({
    type: 'tool-input-end',
    id: toolCallId,
    providerMetadata: providerMeta,
  });
  controller.enqueue({
    type: 'tool-call',
    toolCallId,
    toolName: part.functionCall.name,
    input: '{}',
    providerMetadata: providerMeta,
  });
  hasToolCalls = true;
}
```

The same fix needs to land in whatever code the AI Gateway server uses to parse Vertex's SSE into v3 events. Happy to send a PR for the `@ai-sdk/google` side if useful; gateway-server side I can't reach.

### Environment

- `ai`: 6.0.168
- `@ai-sdk/google`: 3.0.65 (also reproduces on the `4.0.0-beta.47` source on `main`)
- `@ai-sdk/google-vertex`: 4.0.113
- `@ai-sdk/gateway`: 3.0.104
- Model: `google/gemini-3-flash-preview`

### Related

- #13908 carries forward the first signature to sibling parallel calls. That fix doesn't help when the first call itself is dropped.
- Supersedes #14840 (wrong original framing).

## Description

`embed()` and `embedMany()` throw at runtime when used with a provider that implements `EmbeddingModelV2`, because the v2 `doEmbed` return type does not include a `warnings` field, and the SDK assumes it is always defined.

The SDK still accepts v2 models — `packages/ai/src/types/embedding-model.ts` declares:

```ts
export type EmbeddingModel =
  | EmbeddingModelV4
  | EmbeddingModelV3
  | EmbeddingModelV2<string>;
```

There is also a dedicated v2 → v3 → v4 compatibility adapter chain in `packages/ai/src/model/as-embedding-model-v3.ts` and `as-embedding-model-v4.ts`, with a runtime warning logged in compatibility mode. So v2 support is part of the public surface, but the embed code paths assume v3+ behavior.

## Reproduction

Using a `MockEmbeddingModelV2` whose `doEmbed` returns no `warnings` field (mirroring an older provider implementation):

```ts
import { embed, embedMany } from 'ai';
import { MockEmbeddingModelV2 } from 'ai/test';

const model = new MockEmbeddingModelV2<string>({
  doEmbed: async () => ({
    embeddings: [[0.1, 0.2, 0.3]],
    usage: { tokens: 1 },
  }),
});

await embed({ model, value: 'hello' });
// TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'length')
//   at logWarnings (packages/ai/src/logger/log-warnings.ts:106)

await embedMany({ model, values: ['a', 'b', 'c'] });
// chunked path:
// TypeError: result.warnings is not iterable
//   at embedMany (packages/ai/src/embed/embed-many.ts:330)
```

## Root cause

Three sites in `packages/ai` propagate `modelResponse.warnings` directly into the result without guarding against the v2 shape:

- `packages/ai/src/embed/embed.ts:196` — `warnings: modelResponse.warnings`
- `packages/ai/src/embed/embed-many.ts:218` — single-call path, same issue
- `packages/ai/src/embed/embed-many.ts:320` — chunked path, same issue (the spread on line 330 then throws on `undefined`)

Downstream, `logWarnings` at `packages/ai/src/logger/log-warnings.ts:106` reads `options.warnings.length`, and `embed-many.ts:330` does `warnings.push(...result.warnings)`. Both crash on `undefined`.

## Affected versions

- Latest `ai` SDK on `main`
- Any consumer pinning a v2-era provider package (e.g. older `@ai-sdk/google-vertex@3.0.x`) alongside a recent `ai` SDK

## Suggested fix

Coerce `modelResponse.warnings ?? []` at the three boundary sites in `embed.ts` / `embed-many.ts`. This honors the typed `Warning[]` return shape of the embed result and removes the runtime crash without changing observable behavior for v3/v4 providers.

