## Description

When `MockLanguageModelV3` (and `MockLanguageModelV4` on `main`) is constructed with an **array** of results for `doGenerate` (or `doStream`), the first call returns the **second** array entry instead of the first. Subsequent calls are likewise shifted by one. The same off-by-one is present in `MockEmbeddingModelV3.doEmbed` and `MockEmbeddingModelV4.doEmbed`.

The most natural use case for the array form — scripting a deterministic multi-round conversation, e.g. tool-call → tool-result → final text for `ToolLoopAgent` testing — silently malfunctions: the agent never sees the first scripted response and may terminate prematurely, never invoking the tool. There is no error; the output just diverges from the spec.

## Reproduction

```ts
import { MockLanguageModelV3 } from "ai/test";

const model = new MockLanguageModelV3({
  doGenerate: [
    {
      content: [{ type: "text", text: "FIRST" }],
      finishReason: { unified: "stop", raw: "stop" },
      usage: {
        inputTokens: { total: 1, noCache: 1, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0 },
        outputTokens: { total: 1, text: 1, reasoning: 0 },
      },
      warnings: [],
    },
    {
      content: [{ type: "text", text: "SECOND" }],
      finishReason: { unified: "stop", raw: "stop" },
      usage: {
        inputTokens: { total: 1, noCache: 1, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0 },
        outputTokens: { total: 1, text: 1, reasoning: 0 },
      },
      warnings: [],
    },
  ],
});

const r1 = await model.doGenerate({} as any);
console.log("call 1:", (r1.content[0] as { text: string }).text);
//   expected: "FIRST"
//   actual:   "SECOND"

const r2 = await model.doGenerate({} as any);
console.log("call 2:", r2);
//   expected: { content: [{ type: "text", text: "SECOND" }], ... }
//   actual:   undefined
```

## Root cause

`packages/ai/src/test/mock-language-model-v3.ts` and `packages/ai/src/test/mock-language-model-v4.ts` (on `main`) both have:

```ts
this.doGenerate = async (options) => {
  this.doGenerateCalls.push(options);              // length is incremented…
  if (typeof doGenerate === "function") {
    return await doGenerate(options);
  } else if (Array.isArray(doGenerate)) {
    return doGenerate[this.doGenerateCalls.length]; // …then used as the index
  } else {
    return doGenerate;
  }
};
```

After the `push`, `doGenerateCalls.length` is `1` on the first call, so the indexer returns `doGenerate[1]` (the second entry). On the last call, the index goes out of bounds and the mock returns `undefined`, which crashes any consumer expecting a `LanguageModelV(3|4)GenerateResult`.

The same bug is in:

- `MockLanguageModelV3.doStream` array form
- `MockLanguageModelV4.doGenerate` and `.doStream` (v7 `main`)
- `MockEmbeddingModelV3.doEmbed` array form
- `MockEmbeddingModelV4.doEmbed` array form (verified, same code on `main`)

## Suggested fix

Two options, equivalent in behaviour:

```ts
// Option A: index before incrementing (smaller diff)
return doGenerate[this.doGenerateCalls.length - 1];
```

```ts
// Option B: independent counter
let i = 0;
this.doGenerate = async (options) => {
  this.doGenerateCalls.push(options);
  // ...
  return Array.isArray(doGenerate) ? doGenerate[i++] : doGenerate;
};
```

Calls past the array's end probably should fall back to the last entry rather than `undefined` (current behaviour) — small follow-up that would help long agent loops.

## Workaround

Use the function form with a closure counter:

```ts
let i = 0;
new MockLanguageModelV3({
  doGenerate: async () => results[i++] ?? results.at(-1),
});
```

## Versions

- `ai`: 6.0.168 (and reproduces on `latest` 6.0.177)
- `ai`: 7.0.0-beta.116 (v7 `main`) — identical code in `MockLanguageModelV4`, same bug

## Offering a PR

Happy to send a PR that fixes both V3 and V4 mocks (language + embedding) at once, with tests, a patch changeset, and a signed commit — if the suggested behaviour (index from 0, last-entry fallback past end) is acceptable. Let me know if there's a preference between options A and B above.

