## Summary

`@ai-sdk/google-vertex` ships with a `GoogleAuth` cache that, in practice, **never hits** when callers don't pass `googleAuthOptions`. Every call to `streamText` / `generateText` (etc.) instantiates a fresh `GoogleAuth`, which then re-resolves credentials and re-mints an access token. On top of that, when `GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT` is unset, the first call shells out to `gcloud config config-helper --format json` to discover a project ID — adding ~3s to local cold starts.

Combined effect on a dev machine:

| Phase | Observed |
|-------|----------|
| Cold first call | ~6s+ (~3s gcloud subprocess + ~3s misc auth setup) |
| Every subsequent call | ~120ms extra (re-minting tokens via fresh `GoogleAuth`) |

In production on GCP the gcloud subprocess doesn't fire (Cloud Run sets `GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT`), but the cache miss is still ~10–50ms per call.

## Bug 1: cache key uses reference equality, but caller passes a fresh `{}` every time

`packages/google-vertex/src/google-vertex-auth-google-auth-library.ts`:

```ts
let authInstance: GoogleAuth | null = null;
let authOptions: GoogleAuthOptions | null = null;

function getAuth(options: GoogleAuthOptions) {
  if (!authInstance || options !== authOptions) {
    authInstance = new GoogleAuth({ scopes: [...], ...options });
    authOptions = options;
  }
  return authInstance;
}

export async function generateAuthToken(options?: GoogleAuthOptions) {
  const auth = getAuth(options || {});  // <-- fresh `{}` per call when caller omits options
  ...
}
```

When `options` is `undefined`, `options || {}` evaluates to a **new** `{}` reference on every call. The `options !== authOptions` check therefore always fires, the cache is invalidated, a new `GoogleAuth` is instantiated, and the auth/token round-trip is redone end-to-end. The cache is effectively dead code for the common no-options case.

### Minimal fix

```diff
- export async function generateAuthToken(options?: GoogleAuthOptions) {
-   const auth = getAuth(options || {});
+ const DEFAULT_AUTH_OPTIONS: GoogleAuthOptions = {};
+ export async function generateAuthToken(options?: GoogleAuthOptions) {
+   const auth = getAuth(options ?? DEFAULT_AUTH_OPTIONS);
```

This preserves the cache for the (very common) no-options path.

A more thorough fix would be to key the cache by deep-equality (or a structural hash) on `options`, since callers who pass `{ keyFile: '...' }` inline would currently also miss the cache on subsequent calls.

## Bug 2: projectId discovery shells out to gcloud even when the SDK was given a `project`

`createVertex({ project, location })` doesn't propagate `project` into the underlying `GoogleAuth` instance. When `GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT` is unset, `google-auth-library` falls back to:

```
exec('gcloud config config-helper --format json', ...)
```

which takes ~3s on a dev laptop. Setting `GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT` (or `GCLOUD_PROJECT`) in the env avoids this entirely.

I think the cleanest fix is for `getAuth` to forward `project` (when the SDK caller provides one) into `GoogleAuthOptions.projectId` or similar, so callers don't have to additionally set an env var to avoid the subprocess. Happy to send a follow-up PR for this if maintainers agree.

## Reproduction

```ts
import { createVertex } from '@ai-sdk/google-vertex';
import { streamText } from 'ai';

const vertex = createVertex({ project: 'my-project', location: 'us-central1' });
const model = vertex('gemini-2.5-flash');

for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  const t0 = performance.now();
  let firstAt: number | null = null;
  const r = streamText({ model, prompt: 'Say hi.' });
  for await (const d of r.textStream) {
    firstAt ??= performance.now();
  }
  console.log(`run ${i}: ttft=${(firstAt! - t0).toFixed(0)}ms`);
}
```

Run with `GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT` unset and you'll see the first run is ~3s slower than expected (bug 2), and every run is ~100ms slower than it could be (bug 1 — verifiable by spying on `GoogleAuth` constructor or checking how many access tokens get minted).

## Workaround (what we shipped)

A thin wrapper that:
1. Mirrors `GOOGLE_VERTEX_PROJECT` (or whatever your env uses) → `GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT` via `??=` so GCP runtimes' own value is preserved.
2. Passes a single stable `{}` as `googleAuthOptions` to hit the existing cache.

```ts
process.env.GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT ??= process.env.GOOGLE_VERTEX_PROJECT;
const STABLE_AUTH_OPTIONS = {};
export function createVertex(opts: GoogleVertexProviderSettings = {}) {
  return createVertexUpstream({ googleAuthOptions: STABLE_AUTH_OPTIONS, ...opts });
}
```

Measured impact: cold call ~6s → ~1.1s, warm call ~500ms → ~330ms.

## Environment

- `@ai-sdk/google-vertex@^4.0.80`
- `google-auth-library@10.6.2`
- Node 22, macOS

Happy to open a PR for the bug 1 fix if it's welcome — let me know.

## Summary

  `WorkflowAgent` from `@ai-sdk/workflow` fails on the first model call when passed a non-string `LanguageModel` from `@ai-sdk/openai` (or any provider that uses a `url: ({ path }) => ...`
   closure in its internal config). Retries 3× then crashes with:

  ```
  TypeError: this.config.url is not a function
      at OpenAIResponsesLanguageModel.doStream
      at doStreamStep
  ```

  Workaround: pass the model as a string (e.g. `'openai/gpt-4o-mini'`) —
  [`do-stream-step.ts:110-113`](https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/9e8753a26c3ae64c03146302fbd7222b3e8d9faf/packages/workflow/src/do-stream-step.ts#L110-L113) re-resolves strings via
  `gateway.languageModel(...)`. Not viable for users who can't route through AI Gateway.

  ## Reproduction

  ```ts
  // lib/agents/helper-agent.ts
  import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';
  import { WorkflowAgent } from '@ai-sdk/workflow';

  export default new WorkflowAgent({
    model: openai('gpt-4o-mini'),
    instructions: 'You are a helpful assistant.',
  });
  ```

  ```ts
  // lib/workflow.ts
  import { type ModelCallStreamPart } from '@ai-sdk/workflow';
  import { convertToModelMessages, type UIMessage } from 'ai';
  import { getWritable } from 'workflow';
  import agent from './agents/helper-agent';

  export async function chat(messages: UIMessage[]) {
    'use workflow';
    const modelMessages = await convertToModelMessages(messages);
    const result = await agent.stream({
      messages: modelMessages,
      writable: getWritable<ModelCallStreamPart>(),
    });
    return { messages: result.messages };
  }
  ```

  ```ts
  // app/api/chat/route.ts
  import { createModelCallToUIChunkTransform } from '@ai-sdk/workflow';
  import { createUIMessageStreamResponse, type UIMessage } from 'ai';
  import { start } from 'workflow/api';
  import { chat } from '@/lib/workflow';

  export async function POST(req: Request) {
    const { messages }: { messages: UIMessage[] } = await req.json();
    const run = await start(chat, [messages]);
    return createUIMessageStreamResponse({
      stream: run.readable.pipeThrough(createModelCallToUIChunkTransform()),
      headers: { 'x-workflow-run-id': run.runId },
    });
  }
  ```

  Send any chat message → crash.

  ## Root cause

  `OpenAIConfig` stores the request URL as a closure
  [`openai-provider.ts:185-191`](https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/9e8753a26c3ae64c03146302fbd7222b3e8d9faf/packages/openai/src/openai-provider.ts#L185-L191):

  ```ts
  url: ({ path }) => `${baseURL}${path}`,   // closure captures baseURL
  ```

  `serializeModelOptions` strips the function. `[WORKFLOW_DESERIALIZE]` [`openai-responses-language-model.ts:120-125`](https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/9e8753a26c3ae64c03146302fbd7222b3e8d9faf/packages/openai/src/responses/openai-responses-language-model.ts#L120-L125) just calls the constructor with the stripped config, never rebuilding the closure. `doStream` then
  hits `this.config.url(...)` [line 501](https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/9e8753a26c3ae64c03146302fbd7222b3e8d9faf/packages/openai/src/responses/openai-responses-language-model.ts#L501)
   → crash.

  #13779 fixed this for providers that store `baseURL` as a plain string (anthropic, google, cohere, etc.). Providers using the `url: ({ path }) => ...` closure pattern are still broken —
  including all of `@ai-sdk/openai`, `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible`, and the OpenAI-compatible-derived providers (cerebras, deepinfra, deepseek, fireworks, groq, moonshotai, togetherai,
  etc.).


