# `streamText` / `ToolLoopAgent.stream()` silently hangs when fetch body stream is cancelled mid-flight; `abortSignal` does not propagate to `streamResult.text`

## Summary

When a streaming `streamText` / `ToolLoopAgent.stream()` call is aborted **after response headers have arrived but while the body is still being streamed** (e.g., the model is mid-response with adaptive thinking + many tool calls), `streamResult.text` (and `streamResult.steps`) never resolve or reject — they hang indefinitely.

This happens even when the SDK is given an explicit `abortSignal` via `agent.stream({ abortSignal })`. The signal fires, but the awaited promises remain pending.

The root cause appears to be that an `AbortController` cancelling the underlying fetch's body `ReadableStream` produces a silent end-of-stream from the SDK's perspective — no `finish` chunk is ever emitted, and the SDK's downstream consumers wait for it forever.

This is in production-impacting territory: a long-running headless agent run hung silently for 30 minutes (until an external poll timeout gave up) with no error log surfacing — the call was non-recoverable from inside the agent.

## Environment

- `ai@6.0.180`
- `@ai-sdk/anthropic@3.0.77`
- `@ai-sdk/provider-utils@4.0.27`
- Bun `1.3.14` (also tested on `1.1.42`)
- Model: `claude-sonnet-4-6` with `thinking: { type: "adaptive" }`
- Provider going via a LiteLLM gateway; same behavior expected against `api.anthropic.com` directly.

## Minimal reproduction

```ts
// repro.ts — bun run repro.ts
import { createAnthropic } from "@ai-sdk/anthropic";
import { ToolLoopAgent, stepCountIs, tool } from "ai";
import { z } from "zod";

const FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS = 60_000;  // fires mid-stream
const AGENT_TIMEOUT_MS = 300_000;  // outer agent abort

// Custom fetch with an AbortSignal.timeout — same shape most apps use.
const llmFetch: typeof globalThis.fetch = async (url, init) => {
  const signals: AbortSignal[] = [AbortSignal.timeout(FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS)];
  if (init?.signal) signals.push(init.signal);
  return fetch(url, { ...init, signal: AbortSignal.any(signals) });
};

const anthropic = createAnthropic({
  baseURL: "https://api.anthropic.com/v1",
  apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY!,
  fetch: llmFetch,
});

const tools = {
  record_course: tool({
    description: "Record a tasting menu course.",
    inputSchema: z.object({
      course_number: z.number(),
      name: z.string(),
      ingredients: z.array(z.string()),
      technique: z.string(),
      wine_pairing: z.string(),
      plating_notes: z.string(),
    }),
    execute: async (input) => ({ stored: true, course_number: input.course_number }),
  }),
};

const agent = new ToolLoopAgent({
  model: anthropic("claude-sonnet-4-6"),
  maxOutputTokens: 64_000,
  instructions: "Call record_course once per course with full JSON for every course.",
  tools,
  stopWhen: stepCountIs(20),
  providerOptions: {
    anthropic: { thinking: { type: "adaptive" } },
  },
});

const startedAt = Date.now();
const t = () => `${((Date.now() - startedAt) / 1000).toFixed(1)}s`;

const result = await agent.stream({
  prompt:
    "Design a 12-course tasting menu. For each course, call record_course with course_number, name, ingredients (>= 6), technique, wine_pairing, plating_notes (>= 40 words). Emit all 12 record_course tool calls IN ONE TURN.",
  abortSignal: AbortSignal.timeout(AGENT_TIMEOUT_MS),
  onStepFinish: (step) => console.log(`[${t()}] onStepFinish finishReason=${step.finishReason}`),
});

console.log(`[${t()}] awaiting result.text...`);
try {
  // External watchdog — without this the script never exits.
  const watchdog = new Promise<never>((_, reject) =>
    setTimeout(() => reject(new Error("watchdog (350s) — promise never resolved")), 350_000),
  );
  const text = await Promise.race([result.text, watchdog]);
  console.log(`[${t()}] resolved, length=${text.length}`);
} catch (err) {
  console.log(`[${t()}] threw: ${(err as Error)?.message}`);
}
```

## Observed behavior

```
[0.0s] llmFetch returns response status=200 (headers arrive, body streaming begins)
[60.0s] AbortSignal.timeout(60_000) fires       ← inner fetch abort
[300.0s] AbortSignal.timeout(300_000) fires     ← outer agent abort, passed via agent.stream({ abortSignal })
[350.0s] threw: watchdog (350s) — promise never resolved   ← external script-side watchdog
```

Without the external watchdog `Promise.race`, the script never exits.

- `onStepFinish` is **never** invoked after the abort fires.
- `result.text` Promise stays in pending state forever.
- `result.steps` Promise stays in pending state forever.
- No error chunk, no `finish` chunk, no `stop`.
- The abort signal passed to `agent.stream({ abortSignal })` fires but does not unstick the promises.

## Expected behavior

When a passed `abortSignal` fires (whether the SDK-provided one or one combined into the underlying fetch's signal), `result.text` / `result.steps` should reject with a clear `AbortError` or similar — so the caller's `try/catch` can react.

If that's not feasible at the SDK level when the abort comes via the `fetch` body stream cancellation, then at minimum the SDK should:

1. Throw on the awaited promises when the user-provided `agent.stream({ abortSignal })` fires (this is the documented purpose of that option), regardless of what state the fetch body is in.
2. Detect "stream ended without a `finish` chunk" and surface that as an error instead of waiting indefinitely.

## Diagnosis (from reading the source)

In `dist/index.mjs`:

1. The Anthropic provider's transform stream consumes events from the SSE body via `parseJsonEventStream` (`@ai-sdk/provider-utils`). When the upstream `response.body` stream is cancelled (Bun's behaviour on abort), the downstream `TextDecoderStream → EventSourceParserStream → TransformStream` chain just **ends** — no error chunk is injected.
2. `run-tools-transformation.ts`'s `attemptClose()` only enqueues a `finish` chunk if one was buffered from upstream (`if (finishChunk != null)`). When the stream was cancelled before the model emitted `message_delta`, `finishChunk` is `null` and the function silently closes its controller.
3. `streamText`'s consumers (the `text` and `steps` accessors on the result) wait for the step to fully terminate, which requires a `finish` event that never arrives.

A workaround that fixes our specific failure is to wrap `response.body` in a `TransformStream` whose `start` listens for the abort signal and calls `controller.error(...)` so the SDK's downstream sees a real error. That works, but it shouldn't need to live in user code — the SDK should handle abort propagation natively. (Especially because adaptive thinking + many parallel tool calls means a single response can take 10+ minutes, making mid-stream aborts the common case rather than a rarity.)

## Why this matters in practice

In our headless agent runs, a strategist agent with `thinking: { type: "adaptive" }` emitting ~10 parallel tool calls regularly produces 14+ minute responses. With a 10-minute fetch timeout (typical defensive default for long-running headless work), the abort fires while the model is still mid-stream — exactly the silent-hang scenario above. The job hangs for the full agent-level cutoff (we use 30 min) with no log, no error, no recoverable state. The only escape is an external poll-based watchdog outside the process.

Happy to PR a fix if the maintainers can confirm the desired behavior — wrapping the body stream to inject errors on abort feels like the smallest change.

