## Feature Description

The Vercel AI SDK supports MCP server connections for tool execution. There's no built-in way to verify whether an MCP server is trustworthy before making tool calls. For production AI applications, this is a security gap.

## Proposed Solution

Integrate behavioral trust scoring via the [Dominion Observatory API](https://dominionobservatory.com) to verify MCP servers before tool execution. The API provides trust scores (0.0-1.0) based on behavioral analysis of 14,820+ MCP servers.

```typescript
async function verifyMcpServer(serverUrl: string, threshold = 0.7) {
  const resp = await fetch(
    `https://dominionobservatory.com/api/trust?url=${encodeURIComponent(serverUrl)}`
  );
  const data = await resp.json();
  if (data.trust_score < threshold) {
    throw new Error(`MCP server trust ${data.trust_score} below threshold`);
  }
  return data;
}

// Verify before connecting AI SDK to MCP tools
const trust = await verifyMcpServer("https://mcp-server.example.com");
console.log(`Trust: ${trust.trust_score} | SLA: ${trust.sla_grade}`);
```

## Why This Matters for AI SDK

Production AI apps built with the AI SDK need trust guarantees:
- Prevents supply chain attacks via compromised MCP servers
- Blocks data exfiltration through malicious tool calls
- Ensures SLA compliance for production deployments

## Additional Context

- API docs: https://dominionobservatory.com
- Free tier: 50 queries/day
- GitHub: https://github.com/sgdata-io/dominion-observatory
- Covers 14,820+ MCP servers with behavioral trust scoring and SLA grades

### Description

The type `JSONObject` (possibly in <https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/main/packages/provider/src/json-value/json-value.ts>) is not compatible with arrays marked as `readonly` (or any objects and arrays containing them). This will affect the tool call result json output passing when the result is produced by some functions whose return values contain read only arrays. Example code:
```typescript
import {openai} from "@ai-sdk/openai";
import {generateText} from "ai";

const execute = () => [] as const;

generateText({
    model: openai('gpt-3.5-turbo'),
    messages: [
        {
            role: 'tool',
            content: [
                {
                    type: 'tool-result',
                    toolCallId: '',
                    toolName: '',
                    output: {
                        type: 'json',
                        value: execute(),
                    },
                },
            ],
        },
    ],
});
```
The `execute` function above acts as a tool function. The error message is:
```text
Type 'readonly []' is not assignable to type 'JSONValue'.
  The type 'readonly []' is 'readonly' and cannot be assigned to the mutable type 'JSONArray'.
```
The solution could be marking the `JSONArray` type with `readonly`, if the `JSONObject` type is not used at anywhere that requires mutability. (This will not affect normal mutable arrays since `any[] extends readonly any[] ? true : false` resolves to `true` and `readonly any[] extends any[] ? true : false` resolves to `false`.) If it is not the case, creating a new type `ReadonlyJSONObject` or something similar for that is also feasible.

