### Description

## Summary

`MCPClient.init()` in `@ai-sdk/mcp` v2 stores the negotiated protocol version by assigning
directly onto the transport:

```js
this.transport.protocolVersion = result.protocolVersion;
```

This works for the package's own transports (plain property), but the official
`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` client transports (`StreamableHTTPClientTransport`,
`SSEClientTransport`) expose `protocolVersion` as a **getter-only accessor** — writes go
through a `setProtocolVersion()` method. Since class bodies are strict mode, the assignment
throws:

```
TypeError: Cannot set property protocolVersion of #<StreamableHTTPClientTransport> which has only a getter
```

The throw happens *after* a successful `initialize` round-trip, so every
`experimental_createMCPClient({ transport })` call with an official-SDK streamable HTTP
transport fails against any server, however healthy. `init()`'s catch then calls `close()`,
which aborts the in-flight HTTP response — so in tracing this surfaces confusingly as
"HTTP 200 followed by AbortError", with the TypeError as the actual rejection.

The root mismatch is between the two interface shapes: `@ai-sdk/mcp`'s `MCPTransport`
declares `protocolVersion?: string` as a plain writable property, while the MCP spec's
`Transport` interface (which the official SDK implements, and which `MCPTransport`'s JSDoc
says it maps to) models the write as an optional method,
`setProtocolVersion?: (version: string) => void`, with `protocolVersion` as a read-only
getter. TypeScript can't flag this — readonly modifiers don't affect assignability — so
passing an official-SDK transport compiles cleanly and crashes at runtime.

## Affected versions

- Introduced between `2.0.0-beta.37` (not affected) and `2.0.0-canary.56` (affected);
  still present in `2.0.0-canary.59` (latest canary at time of writing). `1.x` is not affected.
- Reproduced with `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` `1.26.0`; the getter-only accessor is
  unchanged in `1.29.0` (latest).
- The assignment appears to come from the fix for #14413 (send the *negotiated* version in
  `mcp-protocol-version` rather than always the latest) — which, ironically, breaks exactly
  the transports that issue cited as the correct reference behavior.

## Minimal reproduction

```js
// npm i @ai-sdk/mcp@2.0.0-canary.59 @modelcontextprotocol/sdk
import { createServer } from 'node:http';
import { experimental_createMCPClient } from '@ai-sdk/mcp';
import { StreamableHTTPClientTransport } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/streamableHttp.js';

const server = createServer((req, res) => {
  let body = '';
  req.on('data', (c) => (body += c));
  req.on('end', () => {
    const msg = JSON.parse(body || '{}');
    if (msg.id === undefined) return res.writeHead(202).end(); // notifications
    res.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'application/json' });
    res.end(JSON.stringify({
      jsonrpc: '2.0',
      id: msg.id,
      result: {
        protocolVersion: '2025-11-25',
        capabilities: { tools: {} },
        serverInfo: { name: 'mock', version: '1.0.0' },
      },
    }));
  });
});
await new Promise((r) => server.listen(0, r));

await experimental_createMCPClient({
  transport: new StreamableHTTPClientTransport(
    new URL(`http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}/mcp`),
  ),
});
// TypeError: Cannot set property protocolVersion of
//   #<StreamableHTTPClientTransport> which has only a getter
```

## Suggested fix

Mirror what the official SDK's own `Client` does after `initialize`
(`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`, `client/index.js`):

```ts
this.transport.setProtocolVersion?.(result.protocolVersion);
// keep the assignment as fallback for transports that model it as a plain property
if (!this.transport.setProtocolVersion) {
  this.transport.protocolVersion = result.protocolVersion;
}
```

plus adding `setProtocolVersion?: (version: string) => void` to the `MCPTransport`
interface so it actually maps to the spec's `Transport` shape, with the built-in
transports implementing it.

